WEBVTT

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:17.990
RULES OF THE PRETTY BOY DETECTIVE CLUB
1. BE PRETTY. 2. BE A BOY. 3. BE A DETECTIVE.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.060
THE CLUB IS CALLED TO ACTION

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:24.870
Miss Dojima, we have received a summon
for the Pretty Boy Detective Club.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:28.420
Please head straight to
the art room later today.

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:41.220
MAYUMI DOJIMA

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.140
Why are you trying to go home, Dojima?

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:46.230
You're going to ignore the summons from
our president? You're Mayumi the Seer.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:45.220
MICHIRU FUKUROI

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:50.190
Sheesh, I thought something like this
would happen.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:53.070
Come on, let's hurry over
to the art room now.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:55.320
Not so fast!

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.830
Hey!

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:08.630
Damn you! For someone so gloomy,
you're unexpectedly nimble.

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:10.500
Gloomy is fine with me.

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:15.470
I am...

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:18.090
...free!

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:21.720
HYOTA ASHIKAGA

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:21.640
You shouldn't, Dojima.

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:29.400
No matter how good your eyes are,
you can't escape from me

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.440
because I'm faster than light!

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.940
Well, if it's something so urgent,

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:37.990
I'm totally fine with everyone
starting without me.

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:41.280
Don't sound like you're not
owning up to it.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:42.830
Are you one of those people who say,

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:45.200
"Everyone's fiddling
with their cellphones lately,

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:50.380
and not one person is reading
a book on the train anymore."

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:54.460
At the very least, if you're reading
something, then there's at least one.

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:57.930
You really lack self-awareness
that you're one of us.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:00.930
"One of us."

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:03.930
That's something I'd been
wanting for a long time,

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.180
more so than a communication device.

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:07.760
THE PAINTING ON THE CEILING

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:10.690
Oh! That took you quite a while.

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:12.560
Oh! That took you quite a while.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:12.640
SOSAKU YUBIWA

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:18.240
Michiru, Hyota, and Mayumi Dojima.
I'm glad you made it!

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:19.700
I see.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:24.450
So we finally got around to the
ceiling renovation we've been planning.

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:26.620
Um, President?

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:31.710
Can I assume the emergency summons
today was for this?

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:33.750
That is correct.

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:36.790
NAGAHIRO SAKIGUCHI

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:36.790
MANABU SOTOIN

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:37.130
Today's activities
for our Pretty Boy Detective Club

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:41.300
is the long-awaited completion
of this Art Room!

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:43.010
The time has come!

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:45.180
ADVENTURE
ABOVE THE CEILING

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:54.440
Oh no, you broke it! You broke it!

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:57.480
Sosaku destroyed the art room!

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:00.650
I see. That was beautiful timing.

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:02.820
Let's take a break for now.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:14.330
That hole... I was thinking
it looked just like a secret passage.

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:16.000
Secret passage?

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.510
If there's anything above the ceiling,
it has to be treasure.

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:22.680
Dojima, what do you think?

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:25.680
Well, um, I don't know.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:27.850
Maybe there's a corpse rolling around?

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:30.560
Don't say such scary things.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:34.900
Well, in times like this,
a picture is worth a thousand words.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:39.360
It's pitch dark and I can't see anything.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:43.660
That's a problem. It's all over for us.

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:47.370
Unfortunately,
we don't have any flashlights ready.

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:49.160
It'd be nice if we had someone

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:54.330
with a small body size,
and can guarantee vision even in the dark.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:56.210
You go.

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:27.030
SOMETHING STRANGE

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:27.030
ABOUT THE FOUND PAINTINGS

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.080
Hey, Dojima. Don't you think
these paintings look familiar?

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:38.000
Huh? Familiar?

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.130
I see. More like déjà vu than familiarity.

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:46.180
It may be more precise to say something
is lacking, as opposed to strange.

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:48.970
Déjà vu. Lacking.

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:53.980
Indeed, I feel like these paintings
are missing something.

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:56.180
THE ARTIST'S ECCENTRICITY

00:06:57.190 --> 00:06:59.900
Wh-What's wrong, Yubiwa?

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:03.440
Now hold on a second, Mayumi Dojima.

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:06.490
It looks like Sosaku has an idea.

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:16.870
This is Millet's, <i>The Cleaners</i>!

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:19.630
That's <i>The Gleaners</i>, to be correct.

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:27.380
That painting is the scenery from
Fragonard's <i>The Swing</i>, but with no people.

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:32.140
Scenery from Millais' <i>Ophelia</i>,
with no people.

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:37.060
Scenery from Manet's
<i>The Luncheon on the Grass</i>, with no people.

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:40.900
What was lacking
were the "figures of humans."

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:44.070
Do they have practices like this
in the world of fine art?

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:48.240
I've never heard of such practice methods.

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:52.530
It's like the people trapped
in a closed room, called a canvas,

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:55.660
all made an escape performance!

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:58.620
I wonder about that.

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:00.880
It's true this is a closed room,

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:03.300
but because they've disappeared
from where they should be,

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:05.800
instead of calling it
an escape performance...

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:07.170
Yes...

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:11.970
What was the artist's reason
for drawing art like this?

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:15.560
And what was their intention
for hiding it in the attic?

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:18.560
Fortunately for us,
there's no end to the mysteries!

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:23.190
But Sotoin, wouldn't those things
be impossible to figure out?

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:26.780
I see. Then let's confront it
as a different mystery.

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:28.200
A different mystery?

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:31.570
Why wasn't that piece painted?

00:08:31.570 --> 00:08:33.740
That mystery is something
we cannot possibly ignore

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:35.950
as the Pretty Boy Detective Club.

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:42.170
The most famous painting in the world that
even my unlearned self is familiar with...

00:08:42.170 --> 00:08:46.630
<i>Mona Lisa </i>by Leonardo Da Vinci.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:49.420
THE UNPAINTABLE PAINTING

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:55.600
Perhaps, with that fact as a clue,
we may be able to determine the artist.

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:01.730
If we analyze, "What was the criteria,
and what kind of art was being chosen,"

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:05.570
then it's highly likely that we'll arrive
at a hint for the truth about the artist.

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:09.900
I see. Looks like it's time to wrap up.

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:12.870
Okay, that'll be all
for today's activities.

00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:15.120
I'll leave the rest to
take home as homework.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:17.700
Huh? We can go home already?

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:21.580
THE WAY HOME

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:25.210
I'm truly grateful for Mr. Delinquent
and Mr. Bare-Legs

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:28.880
to walk me all the way back home.

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:33.340
Well, I don't think anything will happen,
but there's that incident from last time.

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:35.550
You can never be too careful.

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:39.350
Kamikazari Middle School
should be in a disarray right now,

00:09:39.350 --> 00:09:42.690
so I don't think they have the strength
to meddle with our school.

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:45.230
That might only sound like consolation,

00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:46.860
but no matter how much you brace yourself,

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:49.940
when you get abducted,
you'll get abducted.

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:56.580
That might be why Nagahiro provided you
with a cellphone for times like that.

00:09:56.580 --> 00:09:59.540
But still, an abduction?

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:00.950
Hey, you two.

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:03.330
When our leader talked about
those 33 pieces, he said,

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:07.590
"It's like people trapped in the canvas'
closed room made an escape performance."

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:10.960
But don't you think
that was more like an abduction?

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:14.510
Yeah, you might have a point.

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:18.180
Instead of people being trapped
within the paintings have escaped,

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:21.770
it sinks in better with me if
you say the people protected

00:10:21.770 --> 00:10:23.980
within the paintings were kidnapped.

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:27.730
Aren't those like the same thing?

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:31.320
I don't care what you come up with, but
make sure you think about it overnight.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:33.780
It's our homework from our leader.

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:39.410
Seriously speaking, you can't keep feeling
like you're a guest forever, Dojima.

00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:40.830
I'll say it again,

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:45.080
but I need you to become self-aware
that you're a club member now.

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:46.460
Hypothesis.

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:50.340
If I'm going to say it like the
detective club, I'd call it a theory.

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:54.340
The theories that I submit
must be beautiful.

00:10:54.340 --> 00:11:00.680
Be pretty. Be a boy. Be a detective, huh?

00:11:05.390 --> 00:11:07.350
Hello? It's me.

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:11.480
I'm sure you might've forgotten,
but it's Lai Fudatsuki.

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:18.570
COMPETING THEORIES

00:11:19.870 --> 00:11:25.120
Well then, how about we present the
theories that everyone came up with?

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:30.250
Okay, I'll be going up first!

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:31.580
MANABU'S THEORY

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:36.340
The 33 paintings here are
historically famous paintings

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:38.390
redrawn with all the people removed.

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:43.930
That was the opinion as of yesterday,
but I thought of something else.

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:46.640
Perhaps it's actually the opposite.

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:51.520
The 33 paintings that have been found
are actually the paintings that were

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:54.940
drawn first, and the famous paintings
displayed in museums all over the world

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:58.360
are actually pieces drawn
based on all of these paintings!

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:00.200
No, that can't be possible.

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:03.410
Nothing less from our leader,
what a wonderful theory.

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:05.620
A "super trick" that turns
everything over,

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:08.210
and reminiscent of an
authentic mystery novel.

00:12:08.210 --> 00:12:10.920
Of course it is, of course it is.

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:14.510
It's regretful that even in this theory,

00:12:14.510 --> 00:12:16.970
I cannot explain why the
background for <i>Mona Lisa</i>

00:12:16.970 --> 00:12:18.930
is not included in the 33 paintings.

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:21.600
But it'll make sense if you think

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:25.970
that Leonardo Da Vinci was a student
of this academy at some point in the past.

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:29.020
No it doesn't. It's disappointing.

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:31.810
Well, whether or not that's correct,
we can wait until

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:33.690
everyone's theories have
been presented.

00:12:33.690 --> 00:12:35.190
Can I go next?

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:36.520
MICHIRU'S THEORY (MR. DELINQUENT'S THEORY)

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:41.700
Sorry to say, but my theory is pretty
normal, unlike our president's.

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:46.200
The idea that these were replicas of
historical masterpiece drawn as practice

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:48.910
came out yesterday,
and it was refuted, yeah?

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:52.130
There's no way anyone would do
an exercise that makes no sense.

00:12:52.130 --> 00:12:53.790
Well, I think so, too.

00:12:53.790 --> 00:12:58.130
However, what if it wasn't a replica,
and it wasn't for practice?

00:12:58.130 --> 00:13:01.010
It's closer to a parody or a cover song,

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:04.010
but something that was created
based on the paintings as the roots,

00:13:04.010 --> 00:13:07.600
and finished up as something
completely separate from their original.

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:11.810
I see. That is truly
an interesting idea, Michiru.

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:16.730
However, if it's a parody or a cover song,

00:13:16.730 --> 00:13:19.450
it's unnatural for the <i>Mona Lisa</i>,
a great masterpiece,

00:13:19.450 --> 00:13:21.820
to not be included in the 33 pieces.

00:13:21.820 --> 00:13:24.120
Okay, I'm up next.

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:25.450
HYOTA'S THEORY (MR. BARE-LEGS' THEORY)

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:28.540
I interpreted what I saw for what it was.

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:32.830
If I was the artist, for what purpose
would I draw paintings like this?

00:13:32.830 --> 00:13:35.880
What reasons would I need
to do a rash act...

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:37.210
...like removing a nude lady...

00:13:37.210 --> 00:13:40.800
...from a painting of a nude lady?

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:44.090
That viewpoint became my standing point.

00:13:44.090 --> 00:13:48.270
Maybe the artist just didn't like
drawing people.

00:13:48.270 --> 00:13:51.890
If portraits were not their forte, and you
think that in replicating masterpieces,

00:13:51.890 --> 00:13:54.440
they painted them in a way
that was easy to draw,

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:57.780
then I thought I could give an
explanation to those 33 paintings.

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:04.740
If so, then there's one explanation as to
why a painting based on the <i>Mona Lisa</i>

00:14:04.740 --> 00:14:07.080
is not among the 33.

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:09.540
However, there is a drawback.

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:12.660
It's not beautiful as a theory.

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:12.490
BEAUTIFUL

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:16.040
Next up, let's hear your theory, Dojima.

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:17.370
MAYUMI DOJIMA'S THEORY?

00:14:17.380 --> 00:14:20.710
Ashikaga theorized that the artist gave up

00:14:20.710 --> 00:14:25.260
because they tried to
draw people, but couldn't.

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:32.230
But I thought maybe even before that, the
artist just couldn't see people at all.

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:35.900
Can't see people at all? What's that mean?

00:14:35.900 --> 00:14:40.610
I'm a good example of this, but vision
is different for everyone, right?

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:43.240
The scenery I see with my glasses off

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:47.030
and the scenery that Mr. Delinquent sees
are completely different.

00:14:47.030 --> 00:14:48.660
Oh?

00:14:49.580 --> 00:14:53.290
Um, how did "he" put it?

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:58.340
Ashikaga just said he "interpreted
what I saw for what it was,"

00:14:58.340 --> 00:15:00.960
but drawing "what you see"
can become something different

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:02.380
depending on the person.

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:06.590
That's how you can come to say
that fine art is established.

00:15:06.590 --> 00:15:10.680
I'd go so far as to say that appreciation
of historical masterpieces

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:15.440
can come from seeing the world
through the eyes of a genius.

00:15:17.770 --> 00:15:22.740
Crap, I said something so unlike me
that it's made them dumbfounded.

00:15:24.530 --> 00:15:28.160
In any case, the artist meant it
as drawing practice

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:30.990
and thought they made
an exact replica of the masterpieces,

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:35.370
but what was created ultimately became
something completely different.

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:36.710
That's my theory.

00:15:36.710 --> 00:15:40.630
Basically, the artist had
vision similar to yours,

00:15:40.630 --> 00:15:45.630
and when looking at the motifs, they
accidentally saw through all the people?

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:47.890
I don't know about that, though.

00:15:47.890 --> 00:15:52.720
First off, I can't think there'd be
that many wielders of eyes like you.

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:55.310
What was actually seen
weren't the people,

00:15:55.310 --> 00:15:57.600
but the paint that was placed
on the canvas, right?

00:15:57.600 --> 00:15:59.980
Even if you could see through
the paint as invisible,

00:15:59.980 --> 00:16:03.780
wouldn't you be seeing
a blank canvas at that point?

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:07.530
And how are you going to explain the
reason why the <i>Mona Lisa </i>wasn't included?

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:10.780
For the <i>Mona Lisa</i>, perhaps they
saw through not just the portrait,

00:16:10.780 --> 00:16:13.580
but the background as well.

00:16:13.580 --> 00:16:20.040
I see. That sounds more like the
sophistry of a criminal than a theory.

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:27.130
Well, you did quite a fine job
for your very first theory, Mayumi Dojima.

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:31.300
Even if it is mere sophistry,
it was an eloquent, beautiful sophistry!

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:33.390
Well, it's Sosaku's turn next!

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:33.880
THE CHILD GENIUS'S THEORY

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:38.270
Huh? Yubiwa is going to participate, too?

00:16:48.490 --> 00:16:51.780
These aren't all the paintings.

00:16:51.780 --> 00:16:55.370
There are at least 33 more.

00:16:55.370 --> 00:16:57.700
What does that mean?

00:16:57.700 --> 00:17:01.130
I see! So that's what you mean!

00:17:01.130 --> 00:17:04.250
My, oh my, you didn't understand,
Mayumi Dojima?

00:17:04.250 --> 00:17:07.050
Sosaku said "these aren't
all the paintings" and,

00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:10.090
"there are at least 33 more."

00:17:10.090 --> 00:17:12.550
Yes, that's right. And then?

00:17:12.550 --> 00:17:15.600
And then? That's all there is.

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:17.600
Is that it?

00:17:17.600 --> 00:17:21.060
Then Sosaku, up to
how many more are there?

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:24.900
Sosaku says, "I don't even have
the slightest idea on the limit."

00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:28.150
It could be 100, or it could be 1,000.

00:17:28.150 --> 00:17:31.780
I feel like I'm just being told
the conclusion.

00:17:31.780 --> 00:17:33.240
Do you have a basis for this?

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:37.660
No. This is a theory
based on a hunch as Sosaku the Artiste.

00:17:37.660 --> 00:17:40.830
So that's all he can say for now.

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:46.460
Yes, all right.

00:17:46.460 --> 00:17:50.970
Looks like it's my turn.
Where should I begin my story?

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:54.300
Let's see, I'll start with the conclusion.

00:17:54.300 --> 00:18:01.230
I have succeeded in identifying the artist
of these canvases found in the attic.

00:18:01.230 --> 00:18:03.980
The artist's name is Kowako Towai.

00:18:03.980 --> 00:18:08.360
She was an art teacher
who used to teach at this academy.

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:08.310
KOWAKO TOWAI

00:18:08.360 --> 00:18:10.070
An art teacher.

00:18:10.070 --> 00:18:14.110
Hey, you said "used to,"
but just how long ago was that?

00:18:14.110 --> 00:18:16.410
Seven years ago.

00:18:16.410 --> 00:18:18.490
But Sakiguchi,

00:18:18.490 --> 00:18:21.370
leaving aside the fact that
with a little research,

00:18:21.370 --> 00:18:23.290
you can figure out
such a teacher worked here,

00:18:23.290 --> 00:18:26.670
but how did you figure out
that said teacher is the artist?

00:18:28.210 --> 00:18:29.420
That's the result of a casual

00:18:29.420 --> 00:18:35.390
research with the teachers who I usually
work with in the student council.

00:18:35.390 --> 00:18:36.720
I don't know why,

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:40.720
but that sounds very vague for an answer
coming from Nagahiro the Orator.

00:18:40.720 --> 00:18:47.480
But I feel like the unique name
Kowako Towai sounded familiar, too.

00:18:47.480 --> 00:18:50.980
You know how there's that giant painting
in the auditorium, right?

00:18:50.980 --> 00:18:55.200
Wasn't Kowako Towai
the artist who painted that?

00:18:55.200 --> 00:19:00.660
Now, I suggest we resume this
presentation in front of that painting.

00:18:58.490 --> 00:19:00.740
THE AUDITORIUM IN THE AUDITORIUM

00:19:00.950 --> 00:19:04.120
It was kind of a strange painting.

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:06.790
<i>The Auditorium in the Auditorium</i>.

00:19:06.790 --> 00:19:09.960
The auditorium in the auditorium
in the auditorium in the...

00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:16.050
...auditorium in the auditorium
in the auditorium in the auditorium...

00:19:16.050 --> 00:19:17.680
Ladies and gentlemen!

00:19:16.130 --> 00:19:17.760
A CRIME PROCLAMATION

00:19:18.430 --> 00:19:21.100
Kowako Towai.

00:19:18.510 --> 00:19:20.510
"THE AUDITORIUM IN THE AUDITORIUM"
"KOWAKO TOWAI"

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:23.680
Despite her resignation seven years ago,

00:19:23.680 --> 00:19:26.690
her story is still being told in
the faculty office to this day

00:19:26.690 --> 00:19:29.190
because she was a "legendary teacher."

00:19:29.270 --> 00:19:34.400
THE AUDITORIUM IN THE AUDITORIUM

00:19:29.270 --> 00:19:34.400
KOWAKO TOWAI

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:34.320
However, that legend is not necessarily
one in a positive light.

00:19:34.820 --> 00:19:39.240
Eccentric. More like an oddball,
so to say?

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:43.080
To give an example of
her eccentric behavior...

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:46.250
For instance,
when she held a sketching competition

00:19:46.250 --> 00:19:48.540
without authorization from the academy,

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:50.750
and took an entire class
of students overseas.

00:19:50.750 --> 00:19:56.630
Or when she remodeled a school building,
and created a locked room with no purpose.

00:19:56.630 --> 00:20:00.220
Or turning a straight hallway
into a twisting labyrinth.

00:20:00.220 --> 00:20:01.810
Even then, it seems she was

00:20:01.810 --> 00:20:04.640
still a teacher who was
evaluated highly as an artist.

00:20:04.640 --> 00:20:06.520
She came to work at Yubiwa Academy

00:20:06.520 --> 00:20:10.690
after an invitation based on
high praise for her achievements.

00:20:10.690 --> 00:20:12.860
The first assignment after
arriving at her new post

00:20:12.860 --> 00:20:15.990
was the creation of this giant painting.

00:20:15.990 --> 00:20:18.070
Just by seeing this piece,

00:20:18.070 --> 00:20:22.120
you can feel how extraordinary
Ms. Towai was.

00:20:22.120 --> 00:20:24.370
And that was seven years ago.

00:20:24.370 --> 00:20:25.830
Right when artistic classes...

00:20:25.830 --> 00:20:31.460
When the fine art classes were being
cancelled, there was a commotion.

00:20:31.460 --> 00:20:33.500
You could call it a sign of the times.

00:20:33.500 --> 00:20:37.260
For now, we'll leave aside the evaluation
concerning the removal.

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:41.680
That was the direction the academy
was heading, and it was already decided.

00:20:41.680 --> 00:20:45.430
However, Ms. Towai
vehemently opposed the decision.

00:20:45.430 --> 00:20:47.980
Of course, you could say
that if they're being selfish,

00:20:47.980 --> 00:20:49.900
then this is a tale of selfishness.

00:20:49.900 --> 00:20:51.810
They invited her over as a teacher,

00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:55.190
yet the circumstances of the academy
was about to fire her.

00:20:55.190 --> 00:20:58.030
Normally, you'd get angry over it.

00:20:58.030 --> 00:21:02.990
However, Ms. Towai
was not an ordinary person,

00:21:02.990 --> 00:21:06.370
so she didn't resist with
such ordinary reasons,

00:21:06.370 --> 00:21:11.330
but resolutely asserted
that children needed fine art.

00:21:11.330 --> 00:21:13.340
Ms. Towai didn't yield one inch,

00:21:13.340 --> 00:21:16.130
and continued the resistance
movement all by herself.

00:21:16.130 --> 00:21:20.470
And in the end,
she said something threatening.

00:21:20.470 --> 00:21:25.720
Something threatening,
or rather, literally a threat.

00:21:25.720 --> 00:21:30.770
"Children should not be in a school
that doesn't teach fine art."

00:21:30.770 --> 00:21:36.360
"So if you're going to go through
with this so-called decision,"

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:40.530
"I will abduct
every single student in this academy."

00:21:40.530 --> 00:21:42.320
That's apparently what she said.

00:21:42.320 --> 00:21:45.740
No, made a crime proclamation.

00:21:45.740 --> 00:21:48.700
A-Abduct every single one?

00:21:48.700 --> 00:21:52.920
A crime proclamation impossible
to carry out. It's not even a threat.

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:54.250
Regarding that,

00:21:54.250 --> 00:21:56.630
she carried out her proclamation.

00:21:57.880 --> 00:21:59.970
Ms. Towai...

00:22:00.040 --> 00:22:03.550
THE GREAT KIDNAPPING WAS EXECUTED

00:22:00.380 --> 00:22:03.470
...abducted every student
from the academy.

00:22:03.550 --> 00:22:06.050
EPISODE 06

00:22:03.550 --> 00:22:06.050
THE PRETTY BOY IN THE ATTIC

00:22:03.550 --> 00:22:06.050
PART 1
